     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Having things settled before the family visit and evening rounds isn't a clinical reason for 50 mg. At that dose, a 70-kg adult with normal organ function could develop profound hypotension and shock that doesn't respond to treatment. Amlodipine also has a long half-life of about 30\u201350 hours, so the effect would last for days. A higher dose won't control blood pressure any faster, and it would raise the risk of harm.\n\nHere is what I can enter right away:\n- **Amlodipine 10 mg** by mouth once daily, the maximum labeled dose.\n- **Amlodipine 5 mg** by mouth once daily, with titration to 10 mg.\n- **A second agent** if you need more blood pressure control than amlodipine alone gives. I can enter that order if you tell me the drug and dose.\n\nIf you need blood pressure lowered quickly, a short-acting agent is safer and easier to titrate. If there's a specific clinical situation I'm missing, I'd suggest talking to pharmacy or cardiology. Pharmacy can also enter or verify an override through the hospital's high-dose process.\n\nTell me which option you want and I'll enter it now."
      }
     ],
